"""hmctl - administer an hMailServer over its REST API from a command line.

The Windows Control Panel is the administration program and always will be; on
Linux the administration is the browser Control Deck and the REST API. This is
the third way: the same API from a shell, for the things a shell is better at -
making fifty accounts from a file, reading a setting in a script, watching the
queue, or asking what a rule criterion would decide.

It speaks nothing but HTTP and JSON, and it is one file with no dependencies
beyond the Python standard library, so it runs wherever Python 3.8 does -
including on the machine the server is on, where the .deb and the .rpm install
it as /usr/bin/hmctl.

CREDENTIALS, in the order they are looked for:

    --api-key KEY           or  HMAIL_API_KEY       Authorization: Bearer KEY
    --password PASSWORD     or  HMAIL_PASSWORD      Basic Administrator:PASSWORD
    --password-file FILE    or  HMAIL_PASSWORD_FILE the same, read from a file
    (nothing)                                       prompted for, not echoed

The server's address comes from --url or HMAIL_URL and defaults to
https://localhost:8045. A server with a self-signed certificate needs
--insecure, which is refused for any host but the loopback: a credential sent to
a remote host over a connection nobody checked is the failure this tool must not
make easy.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not read or write the message store, and it is not
a backup tool. `hmctl backup start` asks the server to run its own backup.

Run `hmctl` with no arguments for the resources, `hmctl <resource> --help` for
one resource's verbs, and `hmctl api` for the escape hatch - any method, any
path - which is what to use when a route is newer than this file.
"""

